{
  "dom_id": "step-q47361",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069816/assets/fa3045b53d5a91ae7adbf450243338b988239749e4ae7340938943cce4c239fb.png)\n\nAn oil tank is leaking $q(t)$ liters of oil after $t$ hours.\n The graph of $q(t)$ is shown above.\n\nApproximate the rate at which the oil is leaking from the tank when $t=2$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$",
  "worked_solution": "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{q}^{\u2032}(t)\u2248\\frac{q(t+h)-q(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2$ and step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{\u2032}(2) & \u2248\\frac{q(2+1)-q(2-1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(3)-q(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-2}{2} \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of leaking at the instant of time $t=2$ is $0.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}.$",
